WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.300 --> 00:00:03.151
Welcome to Module Four: Deployment and Scaling.

2
00:00:03.481 --> 00:00:07.037
A demo agent is easy; surviving real traffic is not.

3
00:00:07.837 --> 00:00:12.523
Picture an online retailer whose shopping agent runs smoothly for a hundred users.

4
00:00:12.853 --> 00:00:16.003
Then Black Friday arrives, with fifty times the traffic.

5
00:00:16.803 --> 00:00:19.035
First, the GPUs run out of memory.

6
00:00:19.365 --> 00:00:21.066
The culprit is the KV cache,

7
00:00:21.116 --> 00:00:24.755
which holds every token each conversation has already seen.

8
00:00:25.555 --> 00:00:26.999
Then latency explodes.

9
00:00:27.329 --> 00:00:31.652
Long prompts are slow to process, so the first word of each reply takes seconds.

10
00:00:32.452 --> 00:00:36.135
Kubernetes was set to add pods when CPU usage climbs.

11
00:00:36.465 --> 00:00:41.557
But CPU sits at twenty percent while the GPUs are saturated, so nothing scales.

12
00:00:42.357 --> 00:00:46.488
Each new GPU pod takes minutes to pull its image and load the model.

13
00:00:46.818 --> 00:00:49.157
By the time they're ready, the shoppers are gone.

14
00:00:49.957 --> 00:00:52.659
Then a shipping tool slows down, with no timeout.

15
00:00:52.989 --> 00:00:56.374
Workers hang waiting on it, until none are left for anyone else.

16
00:00:57.174 --> 00:01:02.201
The agent retries, its HTTP client retries too, and the load multiplies.

17
00:01:02.531 --> 00:01:05.404
On the busiest day of the year, the agent goes down.

18
00:01:06.204 --> 00:01:08.543
Every one of those failures has a known fix.

19
00:01:08.873 --> 00:01:13.751
Lesson one starts inside the GPU, where each request runs in two phases.

20
00:01:14.551 --> 00:01:17.979
Prefill reads the whole prompt at once, limited by compute.

21
00:01:18.309 --> 00:01:22.077
Decode writes one token at a time, limited by memory bandwidth.

22
00:01:22.877 --> 00:01:26.710
You'll see why the KV cache, not the weights, limits how many users fit.

23
00:01:27.040 --> 00:01:31.491
And how continuous batching and prefix caching win back speed and capacity.

24
00:01:32.291 --> 00:01:37.511
Lesson two scales two tiers: cheap orchestration pods, and costly GPU inference.

25
00:01:37.841 --> 00:01:40.700
Inference scales on queue depth or latency,

26
00:01:40.750 --> 00:01:44.084
never CPU, with warm replicas to hide cold starts.

27
00:01:44.884 --> 00:01:48.589
It also cuts cost by routing easy requests to a smaller model.

28
00:01:48.919 --> 00:01:51.962
And it measures cost per completed task, not per token.

29
00:01:52.762 --> 00:01:55.720
Lesson three keeps the agent alive when tools break.

30
00:01:56.050 --> 00:01:58.136
Timeouts, bounded retries with jitter,

31
00:01:58.186 --> 00:02:01.397
circuit breakers, and a fallback, such as a cached answer.

32
00:02:02.197 --> 00:02:04.644
This domain is thirteen percent of the exam,

33
00:02:04.694 --> 00:02:07.822
and it rewards the right signal and the complete answer.

34
00:02:08.152 --> 00:02:10.790
Next up, lesson one: inside the GPU.
